    "explanation": "To both parties. The driver's own trace measures this trip at 14.4 km (EV-GPS-02828, gps_trace.trace_distance_km — the field POL-RD-01 makes governing) against a booked platform optimal of 9.0 km (EV-TRIP-02828), an excess of 5.4 km and 60.0%. Both POL-RD-01 establishment tests pass (over 0.8 km and over 20%), and at 60.0% the excess is additionally a MATERIAL deviation, so the POL-RD-06 driver_profile check was run. The trace is NOT impaired for policy purposes under POL-RD-07 — 114 fixes, 100.0% coverage, a 10-second interval, no gaps and max_gap_sec 0.0 — so none of limbs (a) to (c) fires, the deviation is established on the trace alone, and POL-RD-08's burden shifts to the driver. The rider's initial burden was discharged: trip_record, gps_trace and fare_breakdown all carry TRP-02828 and agree on the 08:08:20 start. The driver's 'Road works' explanation is tested under POL-RD-13 against the traffic advisory for this trip and nothing else, and no advisory is on file: EV-ADVIS-02828-01 is MISSING, issue code MISSING_ARTIFACT, weight_multiplier 0.0, no payload. POL-RD-03 is therefore unavailable, the road-works assertion carries no weight under POL-RD-08, and the measured 5.4 km excess stands — no closure-adjusted optimal exists. Because the driver cited no advisory identifier, this is not the exculpatory-clause-invoked-against-a-cited-artifact case in POL-RD-10(5); POL-RD-13(1) applies, the missing advisory is a gap in the bundle rather than a contradiction, and the case does not escalate on this ground. No other driver-side excuse runs: the chat log (EV-CHAT-02828) holds 0 messages, so no rider-requested detour under POL-RD-04; there are no pickup/dropoff amendments, so POL-RD-05 does not apply; and vehicle_type is RYDE_X, not RYDE_POOL, so POL-RD-12 does not apply. The fare is METERED with quoted_fare_sgd null, so POL-RD-02 governs and POL-RD-11 does not. On the figures read from EV-FARE-02828: recomputed_fare = (base 3.80 + 0.75 x 9.0 km + 0.28 x 13.0 min) x the surge_multiplier of 1.0 read from fare_breakdown.surge_multiplier + 0.80 platform fee = 14.19 + 0.80 = SGD 14.99. Refund = charged_total 20.66 - 14.99 = SGD 5.67, rounded up to SGD 5.70. That is above the SGD 0.50 de minimis and 27.4% of the charge, below the 90% full-refund share, so the outcome is PARTIAL_REFUND. There are no ERP, toll or airport-surcharge lines (erp_lines total SGD 0.00), so POL-RD-09 adds nothing and the POL-RD-02 figure is neither reduced nor inflated. The POL-RD-06 check comes out clean: deviation_flags_90d is 2 (below 3) and 2/180 completed trips is 0.011 (below 0.05), so no repeat-deviation pattern is established; the POL-RD-06(b) FULL_REFUND uplift and the POL-RD-06(c) conduct referral are unavailable. The lifetime counter prior_deviation_flags (4) is CONTEXT ONLY under POL-GEN-01 and was not used. On the driver's submission that recovery is capped at the SGD 4.00 contested amount: that is rejected. No clause caps a refund at dispute.amount_contested_sgd. POL-RD-02 makes the refund charged_total less the recomputed fare, and the only ceilings the policy sets are charged_total itself and POL-RD-10(6)'s SGD 150.00, neither approached here; POL-GEN-03's labels describe the outcome, they do not impose a cap. No driver compensation arises: the fare was billed for 14.4 km (distance_charged_km) and the trace records the same 14.4 km, so there is no unbilled shortfall of over 1.0 km under POL-RD-04. The monetary effect (SGD 5.70) does not exceed the SGD 8.00 automatic execution threshold, so this provisional ruling executes on issue.",
